nd Gardens is a 24.14-acre (97,700 m2) park with Japanese gardens, located on Banyan Drive in Hilo on the island of Hawaiʻi. The park's site was donated by Queen Liliʻuokalani, and lies southeast of downtown Hilo, on the Waiakea Peninsula in Hilo Bay. Much of the park now consists of Edo-style Japanese gardens, built 1917-1919, and said to be the largest such gardens outside Japan.","wpu":"Liliʻuokalani Park and Gardens","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/LiliParkHilo.JPG/960px-LiliParkHilo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hoʻolulu Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.719,"lo":-155.068,"d":1482,"ds":"Hoʻolulu Park (officially the Hoʻolulu Park Complex; sometimes shortened to Hoʻolulu Complex) is a 56-acre (23 ha) park and recreation center operated by the County of Hawaiʻi in Hilo, Hawaii, east of the Wailoa River State Recreation Area and downtown Hilo, and west of Hilo International Airport.","wpu":"Hoʻolulu Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Merrie_Monarch_Stage_Practice_2019.jpg/960px-Merrie_Monarch_Stage_Practice_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hilo Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.736111,"lo":-155.076944,"d":1899,"ds":"Hilo Bay is a large bay located on the eastern coast of the island of Hawaiʻi.","wpu":"Hilo Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Hilo.jpg/960px-Hilo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"James Kealoha Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.735136,"lo":-155.033872,"d":2793,"ds":"James Kealoha (4-mile) Beach Park is a swimming and snorkeling beach located in the Hilo district on the Island of Hawai'i. It is known locally as '4-mile' beach, as its location is exactly 4 miles from the Hilo town post-office. Showers and restrooms are located there, as well as a lifeguard tower for safety.","wpu":"James Kealoha Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Four-mile_beach%2C_Hilo%2C_Hawaii.jpg/960px-Four-mile_beach%2C_Hilo%2C_Hawaii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Wailuku River","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.727778,"lo":-155.0875,"d":2888,"ds":"The Wailuku River is a 28.0-mile-long (45.1 km) water course on the Island of Hawaiʻi in the Hawaiian Islands. It is the longest river in Hawai'i and the largest in the state by mean discharge. Its course lies mostly along the divide between the lava flows of Mauna Kea and those of Mauna Loa to the south.","wpu":"Wailuku River","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Rainbow_Falls_Hawaii.jpg/960px-Rainbow_Falls_Hawaii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of the Holy Apostles, Hilo, Hawaii","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.70239,"lo":-155.077779,"d":3590,"ds":"The Church of the Holy Apostles is an Episcopal church, located in Hilo, Hawaii, that belongs to the Episcopal Diocese of Hawaii of the Episcopal Church's Province 8. In 2015, the church reported 195 members; in 2023, it reported 240 members. No membership statistics were reported in 2024 national parochial reports.","wpu":"Church of the Holy Apostles, Hilo, Hawaii","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Church_of_the_Holy_Apostles%2C_Hilo%2C_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Prince Kuhio Plaza","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.697222,"lo":-155.063333,"d":3661,"ds":"Prince Kūhiō Plaza is a single-level regional shopping mall in Hilo, Hawaii. It is the largest enclosed mall on the Island of Hawaii. Anchor stores are two Macy's stores, TJ Maxx, and Petco.","wpu":"Prince Kuhio Plaza","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Prince_Kuhio_Plaza%2C_Hilo_-_Outside_View%2C_Sears_and_T.J._Maxx.jpg/960px-Prince_Kuhio_Plaza%2C_Hilo_-_Outside_View%2C_Sears_and_T.J._Maxx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Richardson Beach","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.734722,"lo":-155.013611,"d":4883,"ds":"Richardson Beach (also known as Richardson Ocean Park) is a Hawaii County park located just east of Hilo, Hawaii, on the Big Island of Hawaii. Adjacent is the Lele'iwi Beach Park.","wpu":"Richardson Beach","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Richardson_Beach_Park.JPG/960px-Richardson_Beach_Park.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nani Mau Gardens","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.672222,"lo":-155.054722,"d":6448,"ds":"Nani Mau Gardens (20 acres) are commercial botanical gardens located at 421 Makalika Street, Hilo, Hawaiʻi on the island of Hawaiʻi, coordinates 19°40′20″N 155°3′17″W. The gardens' name, Nani Mau, means \"forever beautiful\". They were established by Makato Nitahara and opened to the public in 1972.","wpu":"Nani Mau Gardens","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Nani_Mau_Gardens_%282918581424%29.jpg/960px-Nani_Mau_Gardens_%282918581424%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Panaʻewa Rainforest Zoo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.6552,"lo":-155.0748,"d":8460,"ds":"Panaʻewa Rainforest Zoo is located in Hilo, Hawaiʻi, United States. This small 12-acre (4.9 ha) zoo is the only one in the United States located in a rainforest. It is operated by Hawaii County and receives its funding from the county and from private donations.","wpu":"Panaʻewa Rainforest Zoo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Pana%27ewa-zoo-hawaiian-homestead.jpg/960px-Pana%27ewa-zoo-hawaiian-homestead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kaumana Cave","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.686829,"lo":-155.130782,"d":8828,"ds":"Kaumana Cave is a lava tube created by a 1881 lava flow from Mauna Loa. The tube has been surveyed at 2.026 miles (3260 m) long making it the 57th longest lava tube in the world. The cave is located on the island of Hawaiʻi near the city of Hilo.","wpu":"Kaumana Cave","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Kaumana_Cave.jpg/960px-Kaumana_Cave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hawaii Tropical Botanical Garden","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":19.810556,"lo":-155.095833,"d":9710,"ds":"The Hawaii Tropical Botanical Garden covers 17 acres (6.9 ha) at the entrance of the Onomea Valley on the coast in Hāmākua on Hawaii Island of Hawaii. The boardwalk descends into a rainforest canopy of palms and tree ferns, opening to winding paved paths lined with over 2500 species of tropical plants, including anthuriums, gingers, bromeliads, heliconias, and over 270 species of orchids. Dan J.","wpu":"Hawaii Tropical Botanical Garden","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/HAWAII_TROPICAL_BOTANICAL_GARDEN%2C_a_beautiful_garden_in_a_valley_on_the_ocean.jpg/960px-HAWAII_TROPICAL_BOTANICAL_GARDEN%2C_a_beautiful_garden_in_a_valley_on_the_ocean.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Hilo Bay is a large bay located on the eastern coast of the island of Hawaiʻi.","wpu":"Hilo Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Hilo.jpg/960px-Hilo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}